1877:Langford sequences have been classically used to build new Skolem sequences. In this paper, we use this technique to gracefully label Dutch windmills with three pendant triangles. However, in our construction, the Langford sequence implicitly contains a pivot, which when pivoted can gracefully label Dutch windmills with four pendant triangles. Thus we can in many cases label the Dutch windmills with four pendant triangles, for Dutch windmills of a large order.
1879:For example, let $S_{8}=(4,8,5,7,4,1,1,5,6,8,7,2,3,2,6,3)$ be a Skolem sequence and $L_{9}^{17}=(24$, 17, 21, 22, 18, 14, 11, 19, 25, 23, 10, 20, 9, 16, 13, 15, 12, 11, 17, 14, 10, 9, 18, 21, 24, 22, 19, 13, 12, 16, 15, 20, 23, $25)$ be a Langford sequence. Now, if we take the Skolem sequence $S_{8}$ and the Langford sequence $L_{9}^{17},$ then we obtain a new Skolem sequence $S_{25}=(4$, 8, 5, 7, 4, 1, 1, 5, 6, 8, 7, 2, 3, 2, 6, 3, 24, 17, 21, 22, 18, 14, 11, 19, 25, 23, 10, 20, 9, 16, 13, 15, 12, 11, 17, 14, 10, 9, 18, 21, 24, 22, 19, 13, 12, 16, 15, 20, 23, $25)$ of order $25.$ If we take that triples obtained from $S_{25}$ and pivot the triples $1,2,4$ and $11$ we can gracefully label the Dutch windmill of order $25$ with four pendant triangles.

